WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.559
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:09.269
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.569 --> 00:00:16.459
Unit thirty-one: Ocean Futures. In this unit, you will discuss threats to the oceans and how to protect them.

4
00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.357
ocean acidification.

5
00:00:20.057 --> 00:00:26.038
ocean acidification means the ocean becoming more acidic as it absorbs carbon dioxide.

6
00:00:26.338 --> 00:00:29.789
Ocean acidification weakens shells and coral.

7
00:00:30.489 --> 00:00:33.699
Your turn. Say: ocean acidification.

8
00:00:36.399 --> 00:00:38.015
ocean acidification.

9
00:00:39.017 --> 00:00:40.076
overfishing.

10
00:00:40.776 --> 00:00:44.447
overfishing means catching fish faster than they can reproduce.

11
00:00:44.747 --> 00:00:47.629
Overfishing has emptied many coastal waters.

12
00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:51.365
Your turn. Say: overfishing.

13
00:00:54.065 --> 00:00:55.170
overfishing.

14
00:00:56.183 --> 00:00:57.869
marine protected area.

15
00:00:58.569 --> 00:01:03.228
marine protected area means a part of the sea where human activity is limited.

16
00:01:03.528 --> 00:01:05.968
Koh Rong has a marine protected area.

17
00:01:06.668 --> 00:01:10.702
Your turn. Say: marine protected area.

18
00:01:13.402 --> 00:01:14.926
marine protected area.

19
00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:17.115
coral bleaching.

20
00:01:17.815 --> 00:01:23.228
coral bleaching means coral turning white when the water becomes too warm.

21
00:01:23.528 --> 00:01:26.410
Coral bleaching has damaged reefs worldwide.

22
00:01:27.110 --> 00:01:30.006
Your turn. Say: coral bleaching.

23
00:01:32.706 --> 00:01:33.812
coral bleaching.

24
00:01:34.817 --> 00:01:36.143
deep-sea mining.

25
00:01:36.843 --> 00:01:40.700
deep-sea mining means extracting minerals from the ocean floor.

26
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.821
Scientists fear deep-sea mining could destroy unknown ecosystems.

27
00:01:46.521 --> 00:01:50.102
Your turn. Say: deep-sea mining.

28
00:01:52.802 --> 00:01:54.035
deep-sea mining.

29
00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:56.643
blue economy.

30
00:01:57.343 --> 00:02:02.315
blue economy means sustainable use of ocean resources for economic growth.

31
00:02:02.615 --> 00:02:05.950
Tourism and fishing are part of the blue economy.

32
00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.917
Your turn. Say: blue economy.

33
00:02:12.617 --> 00:02:13.851
blue economy.

34
00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.037
plastic gyre.

35
00:02:16.737 --> 00:02:21.221
plastic gyre means a huge area where ocean currents collect plastic waste.

36
00:02:21.521 --> 00:02:25.599
The Pacific plastic gyre is larger than many countries.

37
00:02:26.299 --> 00:02:29.857
Your turn. Say: plastic gyre.

38
00:02:32.557 --> 00:02:33.906
plastic gyre.

39
00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:35.848
bycatch.

40
00:02:36.548 --> 00:02:41.276
bycatch means sea animals caught accidentally while fishing for other species.

41
00:02:41.576 --> 00:02:43.750
Turtles are often killed as bycatch.

42
00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:48.031
Your turn. Say: bycatch.

43
00:02:50.731 --> 00:02:51.709
bycatch.

44
00:02:52.717 --> 00:02:54.902
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:55.202 --> 00:02:57.956
Rithy, have you dived near Koh Rong?

46
00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:04.166
Yes. The establishment of the marine protected area has made a real difference.

47
00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:10.006
That's encouraging. Elsewhere, the decline of fish stocks is alarming.

48
00:03:11.317 --> 00:03:13.676
Because of overfishing and bycatch?

49
00:03:14.983 --> 00:03:19.656
Partly. The warming of the oceans also causes coral bleaching.

50
00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:24.250
And acidification makes it harder for coral to recover.

51
00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:28.467
There's growing concern about deep-sea mining too.

52
00:03:29.783 --> 00:03:34.325
Surely the protection of ecosystems should come before the extraction of minerals.

53
00:03:35.617 --> 00:03:39.892
Grammar focus: Nominalisation in academic writing.

54
00:03:40.192 --> 00:03:43.410
Nominalisation turns actions into nouns.

55
00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:49.741
The establishment of the protected area made a difference. The decline of fish stocks is alarming.

56
00:03:50.041 --> 00:03:52.714
It is a key feature of academic English.

57
00:03:53.014 --> 00:04:00.319
Nominalisation lets you turn whole events into subjects, a key feature of academic and scientific English.

58
00:04:01.317 --> 00:04:04.306
Now read and listen. The Ocean in Crisis.

59
00:04:04.606 --> 00:04:10.982
The ocean covers more than seventy percent of our planet and produces much of the oxygen we breathe.

60
00:04:12.283 --> 00:04:15.943
Yet it faces unprecedented pressure from human activity.

61
00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:20.701
The warming of the seas has caused widespread coral bleaching.

62
00:04:22.017 --> 00:04:29.136
Ocean acidification, caused by the absorption of carbon dioxide, threatens shellfish and coral.

63
00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:34.969
Overfishing and bycatch have led to the collapse of several fish populations.

64
00:04:36.283 --> 00:04:40.675
Meanwhile, plastic gyres continue to grow in the world's major oceans.

65
00:04:41.983 --> 00:04:46.584
The creation of marine protected areas has allowed some ecosystems to recover.

66
00:04:47.883 --> 00:04:54.399
A sustainable blue economy will depend on the protection, not the exploitation, of the seas.

67
00:04:55.683 --> 00:04:58.394
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:58.694 --> 00:05:02.876
Sea animals caught accidentally while fishing are blank.

69
00:05:03.176 --> 00:05:07.700
A, bycatch. B, blue economy. C, overfishing.

70
00:05:11.717 --> 00:05:14.715
The answer is A: bycatch.

71
00:05:16.217 --> 00:05:18.286
The noun form of 'protect' is blank.

72
00:05:18.586 --> 00:05:22.622
A, protecting. B, protection. C, protector.

73
00:05:26.617 --> 00:05:28.779
The answer is B: protection.

74
00:05:30.283 --> 00:05:33.455
'The seas are warming' → 'The blank of the seas'

75
00:05:33.755 --> 00:05:37.014
A, warm. B, warming. C, warmth.

76
00:05:41.017 --> 00:05:43.504
The answer is B: warming.

77
00:05:45.017 --> 00:05:47.608
Coral turning white in warm water is blank.

78
00:05:47.908 --> 00:05:53.814
A, coral bleaching. B, ocean acidification. C, plastic gyre.

79
00:05:57.817 --> 00:06:00.745
The answer is A: coral bleaching.

80
00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:06.513
According to the text, what has allowed some ecosystems to recover?

81
00:06:06.813 --> 00:06:12.510
A, deep-sea mining. B, marine protected areas. C, more fishing.

82
00:06:16.517 --> 00:06:19.944
The answer is B: marine protected areas.

83
00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:28.201
Now it's your turn. Part one: How can Cambodia protect its coastal and marine environment?

84
00:06:33.501 --> 00:06:39.784
Part two: Use nominalisation: propose two measures and evaluate one challenge.

85
00:06:46.084 --> 00:06:57.583
The expansion of marine protected areas and the reduction of plastic waste would be effective measures. The main challenge is the enforcement of fishing rules, given the dependence of many families on the sea.

86
00:06:57.883 --> 00:06:59.883
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:00.883 --> 00:07:06.682
Great work today! You learned eight new words and nominalisation in academic writing.

88
00:07:06.982 --> 00:07:12.119
Today's value is caring for nature. Care for plants, birds and our planet.

89
00:07:12.419 --> 00:07:15.234
See you in Unit thirty-two. Goodbye!

90
00:07:15.534 --> 00:07:17.896
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

